---
/**
 * The confidence vocabulary, and the corrections log.
 *
 * This is the page that owns the distinction between the site's two horizons. Every
 * other page quotes only the first: `T = (z·s/e)²`, when your own account would show
 * the effect. The detection horizon — when a study could separate the effect from zero
 * at the design's own resolution — is printed here and labelled here, and the two are
 * never contrasted with each other.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import TimeToKnow from "~/components/charts/TimeToKnow.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Rail from "~/components/Rail.astro";
import Rung from "~/components/Rung.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="How sure we are, and how we have been wrong"
  description="The four words attached to every claim here, what the phrase &lsquo;the smallest difference this test could see&rsquo; means, how long you would have to hold something before your own account could tell you, and eight errors this project found in its own published numbers."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <Rail>Reading this site</Rail>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">How sure we are, and how we have been wrong</h1>

    <MarginNote label="The question">
      <p>
        How much of this should I believe? Checked against <code>docs/research/</code> on
        <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Two halves. The first explains the four words that sit beside every claim on this site. The second is a
    list of things we published and later found to be wrong.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The vocabulary</Rail>

    <h2>The four words</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Why four">
      <p>
        Rungs a reader cannot tell apart are not rungs. The mark beside each word is drawn in ink alone, at four
        intensities, so it survives a printout and a colour-blind reader. Nothing here is ever green for good
        news.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Every claim here is labelled settled, probably, might, or we can't tell. Four rungs, not five and not
    seven, because rungs a reader cannot tell apart are not rungs. Each word means a specific thing about the
    evidence behind the sentence it sits in, and each one carries the same underlying question: what is the chance
    this choice leaves you better off than the named alternative, over thirty years, after costs and taxes.</p>

    <h3>Settled</h3>

    <p><Rung level="settled" /></p>

    <p>No hedge, flat declarative. It follows from a contract, a tax rule, or arithmetic, and the only uncertainty
    left is whether you actually do it. Moving your money into cheaper share classes and the right accounts saves
    about <Figure id="structural-budget" inline />. No range, because there is not one worth printing.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Source">
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/FlanaganSe/kelly-portfolios/blob/main/docs/research/structural-and-tax-edges.md"
          >Structural and tax-aware edges</a
        >, as of <span data-numeric>2026-08-17</span>. Built from fund filings rather than a backtest, and
        measured against the portfolio you would otherwise own.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>If a claim has a probability attached, it is not settled. A 99% chance and a contractual certainty are
    different objects, and merging them is how a confidence scale stops carrying information.</p>

    <h3>Probably</h3>

    <p><Rung level="probably" /></p>

    <p>Roughly a 70% to 90% chance, and the sentence always carries the size and the range. The effect is larger
    than the smallest thing the test could have seen, the range stays on one side of zero, and it survives being
    marked down for how many ideas we tried. One material gap remains, and the sentence after it names that
    gap.</p>

    <MarginNote label="What earns this rung">
      <p>
        Benjamini-Hochberg adjusted p = 0.010, positive in all five predeclared sub-periods, and no sign change
        across a 27-point perturbation grid. One material gap remains, and the sentence beside it names the gap.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The lean toward cheap and profitable companies lives here: <Figure id="tilt-edge" inline />. The gap: 86%
    of it is measured on data starting in November 1990, and nobody has ever run it on years the idea had not
    already seen.</p>

    <h3>Might</h3>

    <p><Rung level="might" /></p>

    <p>Roughly 50% to 75%. The evidence points one way and keeps pointing that way, but at least one thing is
    wrong with the design. The effect is smaller than the smallest difference the test could see, or the window
    is short, or nobody has run it on years the idea had not already seen, or it rests on an assumption a
    reasonable person would argue with. A "might" always carries that smallest difference and a plan for settling
    it. Otherwise it is a guess wearing a lab coat.</p>

    <h3>We can't tell</h3>

    <p><Rung level="cant-tell" /></p>

    <p>The range covers real help and real harm. We print the range and refuse to print a probability, because
    inventing one is the failure this whole scheme exists to prevent. This is the most common verdict on the
    site.</p>

    <MarginNote label="The most common verdict">
      <p>
        Unresolved is not rejected. The window could not have seen an effect of the size it was looking for, which
        is neither a promotion nor a refutation.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>It must never be read as "no". Where a result leans, we say which way it leans. Where an effect of a given
    size would have been visible, we say what that size was. "No evidence of an effect" and "evidence of no
    effect" are different sentences and we are careful about which one we write.</p>

    <p>One rule cuts across all four. Every probability here is a best case, because the arithmetic behind it
    assumes we know exactly how large the advantage is. We don't. That assumption removes the biggest single
    source of uncertainty in the calculation, so the real chance is lower than the printed one, and we cannot tell
    you by how much.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Resolution</Rail>

    <h2>The smallest difference this test could see</h2>

    <p>Returns bounce around. Any difference you measure between two portfolios sits inside that bouncing, and
    before running a test you can work out how large a difference would have to be before the data could reliably
    pull it back out. Under that size, a real effect and no effect produce answers that look the same.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Source">
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/FlanaganSe/kelly-portfolios/blob/main/docs/research/timing-rules-on-the-equity-sleeve.md"
          >Timing rules on the stock holdings</a
        >, as of <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span>. The range around the gap covers zero, so even the direction
        is unsettled.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The moving-average timing rule is the cleanest example on the site. It beat a properly matched control by
    0.74 percentage points a year. The smallest gap that design could have detected was 3.03. The measurement is
    real and the arithmetic is right, and the answer is still that we cannot see. Publishing "the rule adds 0.74"
    would be reporting the width of our own ignorance as a finding.</p>

    <MarginNote label="What it cost">
      <p>
        Applying this rule closed two of the rewards we thought we had measured on factors, and moved six
        verdicts from rejected to we can't tell. Publishing a gap smaller than the smallest one the test could see
        would be reporting the width of our own ignorance as a finding.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>This is why so many pages here end in a shrug. It closed two of the rewards we thought we had measured on
    factors. It moved six verdicts from rejected to we can't tell. And it is why we can say that a century of
    monthly US data cannot settle a question, which sounds like an excuse and is a measurement.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Your own account</Rail>

    <h2>How long before your own account could tell you</h2>

    <MarginNote label="The closed form">
      <p>
        <code>T = (z·s/e)²</code> at <span data-numeric>z = Φ⁻¹(0.90)</span>, the same expression the client's own
        horizon module runs. Forty times the noise is sixteen hundred times the wait.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Take the size of the advantage you are chasing. Take the year-to-year wobble between your portfolio and
    whatever you are comparing it against, which has a name, tracking error. Divide the second by the first,
    square it, and you have roughly the number of years you would have to hold before your own results could
    separate the advantage from luck.</p>

    <p>The squaring is the whole point. Double the wobble and you do not double the wait. You quadruple it.</p>

    <p>Run the same 0.50 percentage points a year of advantage at two different noise levels and you get
    <Figure id="time-to-know-scaling" inline showUnit={false} />. Same edge, same maths, one is a lunchtime and one is longer than
    a life.</p>

    <p>That produces the contrast this site is built around. The fee, tax and account-placement savings come to
    about <Figure id="structural-budget" inline />. They wobble by roughly 41 basis points a year against the
    portfolio you would otherwise hold, so you would know inside <Figure id="time-to-know-structural" inline showUnit={false} />.
    Now run the identical arithmetic on the whole recommended portfolio, measured against a cheap index fund
    holding the same mix. Its edge is <Figure id="central-edge" inline showUnit={false} /> and it wobbles by
    <Figure id="tracking-error" inline showUnit={false} />, so you would know in <Figure id="time-to-know-portfolio" inline showUnit={false} />.</p>

    <p>Four months and thirty-one years, one rule applied twice. Between them sits the whole portfolio against an
    index fund that borrows as much as it does, ahead by 2.20 percentage points a year and wobbling by 6.0%, which
    comes out at
    <Figure id="time-to-know-construction" inline showUnit={false} />.</p>

    <p>Which is not an argument for doing nothing. It is an argument for doing the boring things first, and for
    justifying everything else before you buy it, because your own results are never going to be the evidence.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout>
    <TimeToKnow />
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The other question</Rail>

    <h2>The other question, which sounds the same and is not</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Why this matters">
      <p>
        An earlier draft of this site contrasted four months from the first question with fifty-nine years from
        the second and called it one comparison. It was not, and the mistake is the reason this section exists.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Everything above answers one question. When would your own account show it? A second question sits
    underneath all of the research, and every horizon on this site belongs to one or the other.</p>

    <p>When could a study separate the effect from zero at all?</p>

    <p>The first question is about you: your holding period, your benchmark, and how far your own returns wander
    from it. The second is about the design that produced the claim, and about how many months of data it has, how
    noisy those months are, and how large an effect it could have resolved. A result can be quick to live with and
    slow to prove. It can also run the other way.</p>

    <p>The recommended construction is the sharpest case on the site. Your own account would separate it from its
    control that borrows as much in <Figure id="time-to-know-construction" inline showUnit={false} />. Separating the same gap from
    zero, at the resolution this design actually has over its 427-month panel, would take
    <Figure id="detection-horizon-construction" inline showUnit={false} />. Same portfolio, same data, two questions, and nearly
    five times the answer.</p>

    <p>The lean toward cheap and profitable companies runs the other way about. Against a cheap 65/35 index those
    funds wander by only about a percentage point a year, so your own account would see the gap of
    <Figure id="tilt-edge" inline showUnit={false} /> percentage points a year inside a few years. Getting a study
    to say that gap differs from zero takes <Figure id="tilt-horizon" inline showUnit={false} />.</p>

    <p>We print both, and we say which question each number answers. Contrasting four months from the first
    question with fifty-nine years from the second is the specific error this section exists to stop, and it is
    one we have made.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Corrections</Rail>

    <h2>How we have been wrong</h2>

    <MarginNote label="What caught them">
      <p>
        Mostly the project auditing itself: an adversarial review, a re-derivation of arithmetic somebody had
        already done once, a null-case test whose right answer was known before any data was seen. Two were caught
        by reading a filing instead of a summary.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Thirty-two errors have been found in this project's own published numbers and corrected in place. Eight are
    worth your time, because each one moved a recommendation rather than a decimal.</p>

    <h3>An expected return that was never measured</h3>

    <p>For most of this project's life the pages here quoted a trend-following premium of
    <Figure id="trend-premium-withdrawn" inline showUnit={false} /> percentage points a year and used it to size
    a position.</p>

    <p>Nobody had measured it. It was the 2012 to 2025 stretch's own average return with a 1.50% fee subtracted.
    Then it was restated onto a different basis, on which the same history reads 4.17%, and the fee was charged a
    second time by the model that consumed it. Three errors in one number: a 0.77 point variance drag, a fee
    counted twice, and a point estimate used with no error bar, whose own 95% interval ran from −2.67 to +11.00
    and contained the figure it was being used to overturn.</p>

    <MarginNote label="How it was caught">
      <p>
        1.80 plus 1.50 plus half of 13.23 squared over 100 comes to 4.17, which is exactly the era's own
        arithmetic mean. That is not a coincidence with an innocent explanation.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>An adversarial review found it by undoing the arithmetic. 1.80 plus 1.50 plus half of 13.23 squared over
    100 comes to 4.17, which is exactly the era's own arithmetic mean, and that is not a coincidence with an
    innocent explanation.</p>

    <p>Correcting it flipped the sign of the recommendation. On one basis the trend add-on was subtracting 0.35
    percentage points a year against the same portfolio without it. Corrected, it adds 0.34 against that same
    comparison. The recommended trend weight moved from 20% to 25%. The estimated chance of giving up on it fell
    from 42.3% to 17.2%. The number itself is gone, and nothing replaced it.</p>

    <h3>Multiplying two measurements of the same thing</h3>

    <p>To price a fund that leans toward cheap stocks you need to know how much cheap-stock exposure it really
    delivers. We had two numbers for it. One was 0.410, measured off the fund's own returns. The other was 0.520,
    the share of the textbook version an ordinary investor can actually get, taken from the literature. We
    multiplied them, which gives <Figure id="capture-double-count" inline showUnit={false} />, and used the
    product in five places.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Now enforced in code">
      <p>
        The research code raises <code>CaptureDoubleCountError</code> rather than accepting a capture argument, so
        the mistake cannot be made by hand again. The 0.40 sitting in the edge budget was deleted rather than
        replaced.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>They are the same exposure, measured two ways. Work through the algebra and the second number turns out to
    equal the first plus a small remainder, exact to 4.4 times ten to the minus sixteenth on our own files: 94% of
    the measured 0.520 is that same 0.4891 coming back again. Multiplying them discounts the exposure twice, and
    it produces a number smaller than the exposure delivered by every value fund we have audited, which should
    have been the tell.</p>

    <p>Before costs, that line had been understated by roughly a factor of two. The chain is now three terms: the
    weight, times how much more of the thing the new fund delivers than the one it replaces, times the reward,
    minus the cost. The code raises an exception rather than accepting a capture argument, so nobody can make the
    mistake by hand again. The 0.40 sitting in the edge budget was deleted rather than replaced.</p>

    <h3>Comparing a borrowed portfolio against one that borrowed nothing</h3>

    <p>If your portfolio holds $1.32 of assets for every dollar you put in and the index you check it against
    holds $1.00, you will beat that index in good years and trail it in bad ones, and neither result tells you
    anything about your construction. You take credit for the borrowing on the way up and blame the strategy for
    it on the way down.</p>

    <p>We did this. At the same total exposure the trend route's worst loss was
    <Figure id="levered-comparison-drawdown" inline />, and its return per unit of risk rose from 0.538 to 0.641
    while the borrowed-stocks route's fell to 0.530. Two different answers, and the total-exposure figure everyone
    quotes cannot tell the two routes apart.</p>

    <p>The consequence is uncomfortable and we print it anyway. The single whole-portfolio comparison in the
    entire project that clears the smallest gap its own test could see, the recommended portfolio against the
    investor's original at −0.50 percentage points a year, is mostly measuring 5.4 points of borrowing rather than
    anything about how the thing is built.</p>

    <h3>A floor and a range computed with different error bars</h3>

    <p>The lean toward cheap and profitable companies is the strongest measurement here, so it is the one that
    most needed checking. Beside its range we printed a smallest visible gap of 0.47 percentage points a year. The
    range had been computed with error bars that allow for markets clustering, calm with calm and panic with
    panic. That 0.47 had been computed with error bars that treat every month as a fresh coin flip.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Both were stored">
      <p>
        <code>mde_80pc_power_pp_yr</code> and <code>mde_80pc_power_block_bootstrap_pp_yr</code> are both written to
        the run artifact. Only the narrower one reached the page.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Both numbers were sitting in the run's own output file. Only the narrower one got published.</p>

    <p>On matched error bars the smallest visible gap is
    <Figure id="detection-floor-restated" inline showUnit={false} /> percentage points a year. The result still
    clears it, by 10% rather than by 68%. The data needed before a study could separate it from zero becomes
    <Figure id="tilt-horizon" inline showUnit={false} />. The signal drops from 4.73 times the noise to 3.05,
    landing exactly on the published bar for a result that was found by searching. And one arm of the tournament
    stops clearing altogether.</p>

    <h3>Adding up numbers measured against different starting points</h3>

    <p>The asset-placement headline used to read +38 to +55 basis points a year. It is
    <Figure id="placement-restated" inline />.</p>

    <p>Four things were wrong at once. The range had been truncated, dropping the entire distributed reading,
    worth 18 basis points. A rebalancing hurdle avoided had been booked as a saving, worth 14, on the same page
    that says a hurdle avoided is not a saving. Lot selection and never selling had both been booked, and they are
    mutually exclusive. And 94.7% of the top figure came from one holding's undistributed foreign accrual.</p>

    <p>The arithmetic underneath was fine. An independent re-derivation reproduced every priority, the credit cap
    and all twelve cells exactly. What was wrong was the addition. Some lines were measured against a cheap index
    fund and some against the portfolio you would otherwise own, and those are different questions with different
    answers. The function that aggregates them now raises an exception on a cross-benchmark sum, and the function
    that reports a placement component refuses to return one when its value is negative.</p>

    <h3>Assuming every dividend was the good kind</h3>

    <p>Which account a fund belongs in depends partly on whether its dividends are taxed at the long-term rate or
    as ordinary income. We assumed they all qualified, because that is the standard simplifying assumption and
    nobody had checked.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Re-derived, not re-quoted">
      <p>
        Every placement conclusion that predated the filed fractions had to be worked again rather than restated.
        A location ranking that assumes a qualified fraction has assumed its own answer.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Reading the sponsors' filed fractions took an afternoon. VEA files 66.27%. VWO files
    <Figure id="qualified-fraction-vwo" inline showUnit={false} />. IEMG 34.82%, AVES 44.48%, IDMO 25%, DFIV 100%.</p>

    <p>The assumption had reversed the placement ranking for emerging-market equity. Every placement conclusion
    that predated the filed fractions had to be re-derived rather than re-quoted, which is slower and much less
    pleasant. A location ranking that assumes a qualified fraction has assumed its own answer.</p>

    <h3>Reading a fund's biggest holding instead of its filing</h3>

    <p>MATE gives you stocks and managed futures on the same dollar. Whether that is worth owning turns on how
    much of the stock leg it really delivers, and its February 2026 holdings report shows an S&amp;P 500 ETF at
    49.8% of net assets. Read that line and stop, and the fund looks worse than simply selling stocks to buy
    trend.</p>

    <p>The same filing shows a bet on the S&amp;P 500 through a futures contract at another 61.8%. The stock leg
    is <Figure id="mate-base-leg" inline showUnit={false} />, and the arithmetic flips from bad to slightly better
    than break-even.</p>

    <p>Two quarters later the same mistake was available on a second fund and we nearly walked into it again. CTAP
    shows an S&amp;P 500 ETF at 70.41%, which looks damning until you notice the 32.23% futures position
    underneath, taking the stock leg to 102.64% and making CTAP the most tightly packed fund on the shelf. A
    stacked fund's stock leg is everything delivering stocks added together, and on these products at least one of
    those is always a futures contract.</p>

    <h3>A monitoring bar finer than the instrument that reads it</h3>

    <p>We wrote a rule for ourselves. If the amount of trend the fund actually delivers falls below 0.70, sell
    it.</p>

    <p>The first measurement came back at <Figure id="trend-loading-first-read" inline />. It sits 0.019 under the
    bar, on a range that spans the bar twice over in both directions. The reading neither fires the rule nor
    clears it.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Review date">
      <p>
        The bar should have come from the exposure the fund needs to deliver at a 25% weight just to break even,
        which is 0.19 to 0.27. Next reading at 48 filed months, around <span data-numeric>2027-09</span>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The bar stays where it is, unfired, recorded as unresolved, because moving a bar after seeing the number it
    was written for is worse than setting it badly in the first place. It should never have been a round number.
    It should have come from the exposure the fund needs to deliver at the weight we hold just to break even,
    which is 0.19 to 0.27. We look again at 48 filed months, around September 2027.</p>

    <p>This is the same defect twice. An earlier experiment rejected seven candidate holdings against a bar of
    0.30 percentage points a year when the smallest effect that design could see was 1.039, on a page that had
    already measured and printed that number. Six of ten verdicts were restated to we can't tell. The
    specification was recorded as defective rather than quietly rewritten.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The pattern</Rail>

    <h2>What caught them</h2>

    <p>Most of these were caught by the project auditing itself: an adversarial review, a re-derivation of
    arithmetic somebody had already done once, a null-case test whose right answer was known before any data was
    seen. Two were caught by reading a filing instead of a summary.</p>

    <p>The pattern in nearly all of them is the same. A number got separated from the thing that made it true, and
    then it travelled.</p>

  </div>
</Base>
